"OverallSummary": "This case involves Cova Capital Partners LLC's willful violations of Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) and FINRA Rules in connection with three private placement offerings recommended to retail customers between June 2018 and December 2021. Cova failed to meet its Care Obligation by not conducting sufficient due diligence before recommending these high-risk private placements, affecting at least 82 customers who collectively invested more than $12.7 million. The violations include: (1) For Offering 1 (pre-IPO shares of Company A), Cova failed to verify that Issuer 1 actually possessed the shares being sold or to investigate the reasonableness of markups applied to these shares. The SEC later charged Issuer 1 with fraud for charging undisclosed, excessive markups and not having sufficient pre-IPO shares. (2) For Offering 2, Cova failed to reasonably investigate Issuer 2's management, missing that its CEO was previously subject to regulatory actions related to illegal robocalls targeting seniors. (3) For Offering 3 (pre-IPO shares of Company B), Cova failed to verify Issuer 3's ownership of the shares and to investigate SEC charges against individuals associated with another fund from which Issuer 3 claimed to source shares. Cova also violated Reg BI's Compliance Obligation by failing to establish, maintain, and enforce reasonable supervisory procedures for private placement due diligence. Additionally, Cova violated FINRA Rule 5123 by making a late filing for Offering 1. As a result, Cova was censured, fined $30,000, and required to certify remediation of its supervisory systems."
